    │   TankController; `conditions=` and `after=` keyword semantics in add_transition are transitions-specific; fysom uses 
    │   different callback naming conventions (onbeforeevent, onafterevent) and python-statemachine uses on_transition/cond 
    │   declarative attributes; wildcard '*' source transitions (used for the `trip` transition to FAULT) are not universally 
    │   supported the same way across these libraries; python-statemachine requires states/transitions to be declared as class
    │   attributes at class-definition time, not dynamically constructed in __init__ as done here; self.state attribute 
    │   name/behavior may differ across libraries, breaking the `step()` method's direct checks like `self.state == 'FILLING'`
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three packages provide general-purpose finite-state-machine engines that could, in principle, replace 
        `transitions` as the mechanism driving TankController's state transitions (IDLE/FILLING/HOLDING/DRAINING/FAULT). 
        `fysom` shares the closest conceptual API (dict/list-based event definitions with src/dst and callbacks), while 
        `python-statemachine` offers a declarative class-based alternative. However none of them share `transitions`' exact 
        API surface (dynamic `model=` binding, `conditions=`/`after=` transition hooks, `'*'` wildcard sources), so migrating 
        requires re-encoding the state machine structure rather than a simple import swap — consistent with the file's own 
        docstring calling this a Category C substitution target.

